In the previous lessons, we covered the core concepts of Angular, including components, modules, and services. We also explored the mechanisms of Angular, such as the Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) architectural pattern and the change detection mechanism. In this lesson, we will dive deeper into advanced Angular concepts and best practices.

One of the key concepts in Angular is dependency injection. Dependency injection is a design pattern that allows components to be loosely coupled, making it easier to develop, test, and maintain the application. In Angular, dependency injection is achieved through the use of providers and injectors. A provider is a class that provides a service or a value, while an injector is a class that injects the provider into a component. By using dependency injection, we can manage dependencies between components and make the application more modular and scalable.

The Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) pattern is another important concept in Angular. The MVVM pattern separates the application logic into three interconnected components: the Model, the View, and the ViewModel. The Model represents the data, the View is the user interface, and the ViewModel acts as an intermediary, exposing the data as observable properties. By using the MVVM pattern, we can build data-driven applications with complex business logic. For example, in a simple todo list application, the Model can represent the todo items, the View can be the user interface that displays the todo items, and the ViewModel can expose the todo items as observable properties.

In addition to dependency injection and the MVVM pattern, there are several best practices that we can follow to build efficient and scalable Angular applications. One of the best practices is to use a modular architecture, where each module represents a feature or a functionality of the application. Another best practice is to use a consistent naming convention and to follow the principle of separation of concerns. By following these best practices, we can make the application more maintainable and scalable.

Another important concept in Angular is the concept of lifecycle hooks. Lifecycle hooks are methods that are called at different stages of a component's lifecycle, such as when the component is initialized or when it is destroyed. By using lifecycle hooks, we can perform tasks such as initializing data or cleaning up resources. For example, we can use the `ngOnInit` lifecycle hook to initialize data when a component is initialized.

In Angular, we can also use pipes to transform data. Pipes are classes that implement the `PipeTransform` interface and are used to transform data in a template. For example, we can use the `date` pipe to format a date in a template. By using pipes, we can make the application more flexible and reusable.

